[Chapter "Key ideas: Plans for White"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "ideas"]

{Three lines where White's plan is more than normal development. Each board starts from the first move: step through to the position, then the plan.}
*

[Chapter "Space with h4-h5"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]

{Classical Variation, Main Line: 6.h4. White chases Black's bishop with the h-pawn and gains space on the kingside. The pawn on h5 also fixes Black's h6 pawn, a target for later. White then trades the light-squared bishops with Bd3 and castles queenside.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 5. Ng3 Bg6 {[%cal Gh2h4,Gh4h5] The idea starts from this position.}
6. h4 {Threatens h5, which would drive the bishop off g6.} 6... h6 {Makes room for the bishop on h7.}
7. Nf3 {Develops; h5 can wait a move.} 7... Nd7 {Keeps White's knight out of e5.}
8. h5 {[%csl Yh5,Yh6] Gains space and fixes the h6 pawn.} 8... Bh7 {The bishop's only good square (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "h3 and the bishop pair"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]

{Two Knights Attack, Mindeno Variation: 3...Bg4. Black pins the f3 knight, and White asks the bishop to decide with h3. After ...Bxf3 White has the two bishops; Black answers with pawns on light squares, where White's light-squared bishop can't attack them.}
1. e4 c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] The idea starts from this position.}
4. h3 {Asks the bishop to take or retreat.} 4... Bxf3 {Retreating with 4...Bh5 loses time after exd5 and g4.}
5. Qxf3 {White has the bishop pair.} 5... e6 {[%csl Yc6,Yd5,Ye6] Black's pawns go to light squares (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Develop and castle before anything else"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]

{Maróczy Variation (Fantasy): 3...dxe4. After 4.fxe4 White has two center pawns, but f3 has opened the diagonals to White's king. Develop the kingside pieces and castle before grabbing anything: 5.dxe5? Qh4+ is in the Traps section.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 dxe4 4. fxe4 e5 {[%cal Ge5d4] The idea starts from this position.}
5. Nf3 {[%cal Gf3e5,Gf3h4] Attacks e5 and covers h4.} 5... Bg4 {Pins the knight.}
6. Bc4 {Develops and aims at f7.} 6... Nd7 {Supports e5.}
7. O-O {The king is safe, and the rook gets the half-open f-file (Stockfish 0.0, equal).} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Plans for Black"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "ideas"]

{Two lines where Black's plan is more than normal development. Black's plans in the Advance, the Classical, the Exchange and the Panov come from their pawn structures: see Key ideas: Pawn structures. Each board starts from the first move.}
*

[Chapter "Trade knights, then gain time on the queen"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]

{Two Knights Attack: 3...dxe4. White's queen comes to e2 and then e4, so Black trades a pair of knights and brings the other knight to f6 with a gain of time on the queen. Careful: with the queen on e2, ...Nbd7?? allows Nd6 mate (see Key ideas: Typical tactics and traps).}
1. e4 c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 {The idea starts from this position.}
4... Nf6 {Offers the knight trade.} 5. Qe2 {[%cal Ge2e7] Guards the knight and pins Black's e-pawn.} 5... Nxe4 {Trades.}
6. Qxe4 {Takes back.} 6... Nd7 {[%cal Gd7f6] Heads for f6.}
7. Bc4 {Develops.} 7... Nf6 {[%cal Gf6e4] Hits the queen, so White must spend another move on it (Stockfish +0.4, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Doubled pawns for fast development"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]

{Tartakower Variation: 5...exf6. Black takes back with the e-pawn and gets doubled f-pawns, but the bishops come out at once and the e-file opens for a rook. Black's king is safe behind the f-pawns; in the endgame, White's healthier pawns count more.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 5. Nxf6+ exf6 {[%csl Yf6,Yf7] The idea starts from this position.}
6. Nf3 {Develops.} 6... Bd6 {The bishop is out at once.}
7. Bd3 {Develops.} 7... O-O {The king is safe behind the f-pawns.}
8. O-O {Castles.} 8... Re8 {[%cal Ge8e1] The rook takes the open e-file.}
9. c3 {Supports d4.} 9... Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] Pins the knight. Black's pieces are active (Stockfish 0.0, equal).} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Pawn structures"]
[Side "both"]
[Type "ideas"]

{Four structures cover most Caro-Kann middlegames. Each board starts from the first move, reaches the structure, then shows the plan that goes with it.}
*

[Chapter "The Advance chain: the attack on d4"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]

{Advance, Main line, Short Variation: 4.Nf3. White's pawns on d4 and e5 form a chain, and d4 is its base. Black has already struck at it with ...c5 and traded on d4, so no White pawn can defend it. Now the knights and ...Bxf3, which removes a defender, hit d4; White holds it and uses the extra space.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 4. Nf3 e6 5. Be2 c5 6. c3 Nc6 7. O-O cxd4 8. cxd4 {[%csl Yd4,Ye5] [%cal Gc6d4] The idea starts from this position.}
8... Nge7 {[%cal Ge7f5] Heads for f5.} 9. Nc3 {Develops.} 9... Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] Hits the knight that guards d4.}
10. h3 {Asks the bishop.} 10... Bxf3 {Removes a defender of d4.}
11. Bxf3 {Takes back.} 11... Nf5 {[%cal Gf5d4,Gc6d4] Two knights hit d4 (Stockfish +0.1, equal).} *

[Chapter "The Classical structure: the ...c5 break"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]

{Classical Variation: 4...Bf5. White has no e-pawn and Black has no d-pawn, so White's d4 pawn faces Black's pawns on c6 and e6. White has a little more space. Black frees the game with the break ...c5 (or ...e5), which trades off White's center pawn.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. Nf3 Nd7 7. Bd3 Bxd3 8. Qxd3 Ngf6 9. O-O e6 10. Re1 Be7 11. c4 O-O 12. Bf4 Re8 {[%csl Yd4,Yc6,Ye6] [%cal Gc6c5] The idea starts from this position.}
13. a3 {Keeps Black's pieces off b4.} 13... c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] The break.}
14. h3 {Gives the king an escape square on h2.} 14... cxd4 {Trades off White's center pawn.}
15. Nxd4 {Black's pieces have room, and the position is level (Stockfish 0.0, equal).} *

[Chapter "The Carlsbad: the minority attack"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]

{Exchange Variation: 4.Bd3. Black has already traded White's best bishop with ...Bd6. Now Black's two queenside pawns (a and b) attack White's three: ...b5 and ...b4 aim to leave White a weak pawn on c3. White's play is on the kingside, with Ne5 and f4.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. Bd3 Nc6 5. c3 Nf6 6. Bf4 Bg4 7. Qb3 Qd7 8. Nd2 e6 9. Ngf3 Bd6 10. Bxd6 Qxd6 11. O-O O-O 12. Rae1 {[%cal Gb7b5,Gb5b4] The idea starts from this position.}
12... Bxf3 {Trades the bishop for a knight.} 13. Nxf3 {Takes back.} 13... Rfb8 {[%cal Gb8b5] The rook supports the b-pawn.}
14. Ne5 {The knight jumps to e5.} 14... b5 {The minority attack starts.}
15. f4 {[%cal Gf4f5] White's play is on the kingside.} 15... b4 {[%cal Gb4c3] ...bxc3 will leave White a weak pawn (Stockfish 0.0, equal).} *

[Chapter "The isolated d-pawn: blockade and trade"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]

{Panov Attack: 4.c4. After the trades on d5, White's d4 pawn has no pawn beside it. It gives White's pieces space and the e5 square, but it is weak in the endgame. So Black blockades it on d5 and trades pieces, and White should try to keep them on.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. c4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nc6 6. Nf3 Bg4 7. cxd5 Nxd5 8. Be2 e6 9. O-O Be7 10. h3 Bh5 {[%csl Yd4,Yd5] The idea starts from this position.}
11. Ne5 {[%cal Ge5c6] The knight uses e5. Stockfish prefers 11.Qb3, which keeps the pieces on.} 11... Bxe2 {Trades.} 12. Qxe2 {Takes back.} 12... O-O {Castles.}
13. Nxd5 {Another trade.} 13... Qxd5 {The queen blockades d5.}
14. Nxc6 {And another.} 14... Qxc6 {[%csl Yd4] Two pairs of minor pieces are off, and the d4 pawn is a target (Stockfish −0.2, equal).} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Typical tactics and traps"]
[Side "both"]
[Type "ideas"]

{Three patterns behind the traps in this course. Each board starts from the first move; the Traps section has every trap in full.}
*

[Chapter "Smothered mate on d6"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]

{Two Knights Attack: 5.Qe2. With White's queen on e2, Black's e7 pawn is pinned to the king, so a knight on d6 can't be taken. If Black blocks the king in with ...Nbd7, Nd6 is mate. The same mate follows Karpov Variation: 5...Ngf6?? 6.Nd6#.}
1. e4 c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 {The idea starts from this position.}
5. Qe2 {[%cal Ge2e7] The queen pins the e7 pawn.} 5... Nbd7?? {Natural, but it takes d7 from the king.}
6. Nd6# {[%cal Ge4d6] Smothered mate: Black's own pieces box in the king.} *

[Chapter "The f7 square"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]

{While Black's king is on e8, f7 is guarded only by the king. A bishop on c4 and a knight on e5 or g5 aim at it. In the Two Knights Attack, 8.Ne5 even leaves the queen hanging, because 8...Nxe4?? 9.Bxf7# is mate. The same target decides Rasa-Studier Gambit: 6...Nf6? 7.Bxf7+ Kxf7 8.Ne5+.}
1. e4 c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 5. Qe2 Nxe4 6. Qxe4 Nd7 7. Bc4 Nf6 {[%cal Gc4f7] The idea starts from this position.}
8. Ne5 {[%cal Gc4f7,Ge5f7] Leaves the queen hanging.} 8... Nxe4?? {8...e6 is correct: it blocks the bishop's line to f7.}
9. Bxf7# {The knight on e5 covers d7, so Black's king has no square.} *

[Chapter "The dark squares after f3"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]

{Maróczy Variation (Fantasy): 3...e5. The move f3 leaves f2 empty and opens two diagonals to White's king: e1-h4 and g1-a7. In this gambit Black aims the queen and the bishop at g1 and f2. White's best defense is 5.Nc3! and 6.Na4, hitting both pieces.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 e5 4. dxe5 {[%csl Yf2,Yg1] The idea starts from this position.}
4... Qb6 {[%cal Gb6g1] Eyes g1 and makes room for ...Bc5.} 5. exd5? {Grabs a second pawn. 5.Nc3! is the best defense.} 5... Bc5 {[%cal Gc5g1,Gb6g1] Two pieces hit g1.}
6. Ne2?? {The most common reply, and it loses at once. Only 6.Nh3! keeps White in the game.} 6... Bf2+ {The bishop checks from f2.}
7. Kd2 {The only square.} 7... Qe3# {Mate: the king has no square.} *

[Chapter "Advance, Main line, Short Variation: 4.Nf3"]
[Side "both"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Advance Variation, Short Variation"]
[ECO "B12"]

{Nigel Short's calm system: White develops with Nf3 and Be2 and castles before anything sharp. Black strikes at d4 with ...c5, ...Nc6 and a knight on f5.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 {This is the Main line: the bishop gets out before ...e6.}
4. Nf3 {This is the Short Variation.} e6
5. Be2 {Prepares to castle. 5.Bd3 offers a bishop trade and leads to Advance, Main line: 4.Bd3.} c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Black strikes at the base of the chain.}
6. c3 Nc6 7. O-O cxd4 8. cxd4 {Now no White pawn can defend d4.} Nge7 {[%cal Ge7f5] Heads for f5.}
9. Nc3 Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] Hits the knight that guards d4: ...Bxf3 will remove it.}
10. h3 Bxf3 11. Bxf3 Nf5 {[%cal Gf5d4,Gc6d4] Two knights hit d4 (Stockfish +0.1, equal).} *

[Chapter "Advance, Main line: 4.Bd3"]
[Side "both"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Advance Variation"]
[ECO "B12"]

{White offers to trade the light-squared bishops at once, a popular choice at club level. Black accepts and gets a solid structure without a bad bishop.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5
4. Bd3 {Offers the bishop trade.} Bxd3 5. Qxd3 e6 {With the bishops off, ...e6 shuts nothing in.}
6. Nf3 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits d4.} 7. c3 Nc6
8. O-O Qb6 {[%cal Gb6b2,Gb6d4] Hits d4 and b2.}
9. b3 cxd4 10. cxd4 Nge7 {[%cal Ge7f5] Heads for f5 to hit d4 again (Stockfish +0.2, equal).} *

[Chapter "Advance, Botvinnik-Carls Defense: 3...c5"]
[Side "both"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Advance Variation, Botvinnik-Carls Defense"]
[ECO "B12"]

{Black hits the base of White's chain at once. White supports d4 with c3; Black takes on d4, pins with ...Bg4 and brings a knight to f5, so Black's pieces gang up on d4.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5
3. e5 {This is the Advance: White gains space and takes f6 away from Black's knight.} c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] This is the Botvinnik-Carls Defense: Black hits d4 at once.}
4. c3 {Supports the chain. 4.dxc5 is under Advanced variations.} Nc6 {Adds pressure on d4.}
5. Nf3 {The most common move.} cxd4 {Takes first: 5...Bg4 at once allows 6.dxc5 (Stockfish +0.5).}
6. cxd4 {Now no White pawn can defend d4.} Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] Pins the knight that guards d4.}
7. Be2 e6 {With the bishop outside the chain, the e-pawn can move.}
8. O-O Nge7 {[%cal Ge7f5] Heads for f5.}
9. Nbd2 Nf5 {[%cal Gf5d4,Gc6d4] Two knights hit d4, and Black's plan is easy to follow (Stockfish +0.4, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Panov Attack: 4.c4"]
[Side "both"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Panov Attack"]
[ECO "B13"]

{White hits d5 again and often ends up with an isolated d-pawn. Black develops quickly, blockades d5 with a knight and trades pieces.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5
4. c4 {This is the Panov Attack.} Nf6 {Defends d5 and develops.}
5. Nc3 Nc6 6. Nf3 Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] Pins the knight that guards d4.}
7. cxd5 Nxd5 {The knight blockades d5.}
8. Be2 e6 9. O-O Be7
10. h3 Bh5 {Black keeps the bishop on the f3 knight, and White's d-pawn is isolated (Stockfish +0.1, equal).} *

[Chapter "Classical Variation: 4...Bf5"]
[Side "both"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Classical Variation"]
[ECO "B18"]

{The main line of the Caro-Kann: Black develops the bishop at once and lets White chase it. Club players often meet it with Nf3, Bd3 and a quick bishop trade instead of the sharp h4.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5
3. Nc3 {Defends e4. 3.Nd2 does the same job.} dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 {[%cal Gf5e4] The bishop comes out before ...e6 and hits the knight.}
5. Ng3 Bg6 {The bishop keeps its diagonal.}
6. Nf3 {6.h4 is under Advanced variations.} Nd7 {Guards e5 and prepares ...Ngf6.}
7. Bd3 Bxd3 8. Qxd3 Ngf6
9. O-O e6 {With the bishops traded, ...e6 shuts nothing in.}
10. Re1 Be7 11. c4 O-O
12. Bf4 Re8 {[%cal Gc6c5,Ge6e5] Black is solid and frees the game later with ...c5 or ...e5 (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Tartakower Variation: 5...exf6"]
[Side "both"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Tartakower Variation"]
[ECO "B15"]

{Black accepts doubled f-pawns in return for quick development. The bishops come out at once, and the bishop pins the f3 knight.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 {Offers the knight trade.}
5. Nxf6+ exf6 {This is the Tartakower Variation. The f-pawns are doubled, but Black's bishops are free.}
6. Nf3 Bd6 7. Bd3 O-O 8. O-O Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] Pins the knight.}
9. h3 Bh5 10. c3 Nd7 {Black has an active game; White has the healthier pawns (Stockfish +0.1, equal).} *

[Chapter "Two Knights Attack: 3...dxe4"]
[Side "both"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Two Knights Attack"]
[ECO "B10"]

{White develops both knights before deciding on d4, and the lines are full of traps. With the queen on e2 and a bishop on c4, White aims at e7 and f7.}
1. e4 c6
2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 {This is the Two Knights Attack. 2.Nf3 d5 3.Nc3 reaches the same position, and more club games arrive that way.} dxe4
4. Nxe4 Nf6 {Offers the knight trade.}
5. Qe2 {[%cal Ge2e7] Guards the knight and pins Black's e-pawn: 5...Nbd7?? 6.Nd6 is mate.} Nxe4
6. Qxe4 Nd7 {Aims for ...Nf6 with a gain of time on the queen.}
7. Bc4 Nf6 {Hits the queen.}
8. Ne5 {[%cal Gc4f7,Ge5f7] A trap: the queen is left hanging, but 8...Nxe4?? 9.Bxf7# is mate.} e6 {Blocks the bishop's line to f7.}
9. Qe2 Bd6 10. d4 O-O 11. O-O {Black is solid; White's pieces are a little more active (Stockfish +0.4, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Maróczy Variation: 3...dxe4"]
[Side "both"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Maróczy Variation"]
[ECO "B12"]

{After 3...dxe4 4.fxe4, White has two center pawns and the half-open f-file, but f3 has weakened the diagonals to White's king. Black takes on e4 and hits back with ...e5 at once.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 {This is the Maróczy Variation, also called the Fantasy.} dxe4 4. fxe4 e5 {[%cal Ge5d4,Rd8h4] Strikes at d4 before White is developed. 5.dxe5? runs into ...Qh4+: see the Traps section.}
5. Nf3 {Attacks e5 and covers h4. White's other fifth moves are under Advanced.}
5... Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] Pins the knight that guards d4.}
6. Bc4 {Aims at f7.}
6... Nd7 {Supports e5 and prepares ...Ngf6.}
7. O-O Ngf6 {Not 7...Bxf3? 8.Qxf3, when f7 comes under fire.}
8. c3 Bd6 {Black has developed all four minor pieces and keeps a strong pawn on e5 (Stockfish −0.2, equal).} *

[Chapter "Maróczy Variation: 3...e6"]
[Side "both"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Maróczy Variation"]
[ECO "B12"]

{Black keeps the center closed with 3...e6, a French-style setup in which f3 looks odd. Black pins the c3 knight with ...Bb4, and White answers with a3 and Bd3.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5
3. f3 {This is the Maróczy Variation, also called the Fantasy. The f-pawn guards e4.} e6 {Solid: Black keeps the center closed.}
4. Nc3 {Defends e4 a second time.} Bb4 {[%cal Gb4c3] Pins the knight that guards e4.}
5. Bd2 {Unpins, so the knight can take back on e4.} Ne7 {Develops the knight where e5 can't hit it.}
6. a3 {Asks the bishop to decide.} Ba5 {Keeps the bishop aimed at c3.}
7. Bd3 {Guards e4.} Bb6 {[%cal Gb6d4] The bishop eyes d4.}
8. Nge2 O-O {Both sides finish developing (Stockfish +0.2, equal).} *

[Chapter "Trap, Short Variation: 7...Qxb2? 8.Qb1!"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "7...Qxb2"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Advance Variation, Short Variation"]
[ECO "B12"]

{In the Short Variation, White can guard d4 with 6.Be3. Black's queen on b6 then eyes b2, but taking it before Black has developed the kingside hands White the initiative.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 4. Nf3 e6 5. Be2 c5 6. Be3 Qb6 7. Nc3 Qxb2? {Grabs the pawn too early.}
8. Qb1! {[%cal Gb1b2] Offers a queen trade that Black can't avoid without losing more.} Qxb1+ {8...Qb4 9.a3 Qxb1+ 10.Rxb1 reaches the same endgame. 8...Qb6? 9.dxc5 Bxc5 10.Bb5+ catches Black's king in the center (Stockfish +1.4, clearly better for White).}
9. Rxb1 c4 10. Rxb7 {White has won back the pawn, and the rook on b7 is very active (Stockfish +0.8, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Trap, Two Knights Attack: 5...Nbd7?? 6.Nd6#"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Two Knights Attack"]
[ECO "B10"]

{The best-known trap in the Caro-Kann. With White's queen on e2, Black's e7 pawn is pinned to the king, so a knight on d6 can't be taken.}
1. e4 c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6
5. Qe2 {[%cal Ge2e7] The queen pins Black's e-pawn.} Nbd7?? {Natural: Black wants to take back on f6 with a knight.}
6. Nd6# {[%cal Ge4d6] Smothered mate. The e7 pawn is pinned, and Black's own pieces block every escape square.} *

[Chapter "Trap, Two Knights Attack: 8...Bg4? 9.Ne5!"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Two Knights Attack"]
[ECO "B10"]

{This comes after 8.Qe2, White's main alternative to 8.Ne5 in Two Knights Attack: 3...dxe4. With Black's king on e8 and the bishop on g4, the pinned knight can still jump to e5, because it threatens mate on f7.}
1. e4 c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 5. Qe2 Nxe4 6. Qxe4 Nd7 7. Bc4 Nf6 8. Qe2 Bg4? {Pins the knight, but f7 is weak. 8...Bf5 is correct.}
9. Ne5! {[%cal Ge5g4,Gc4f7] Hits the bishop and threatens Bxf7 mate, since the knight covers d7.} Be6 {The only way to stop the mate and keep the bishop. 9...Qd4? 10.Bxf7+ Kd8 11.f3 traps the bishop on g4 (Stockfish +3.9, winning for White).}
10. Bxe6 fxe6 11. O-O {Black's e-pawns are weak, and the king is stuck in the center (Stockfish +2.6, winning for White).} *

[Chapter "Trap, Two Knights Attack: 7...Bh7? 8.Qh5!"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "8.Qh5"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Two Knights Attack"]
[ECO "B10"]

{In the Two Knights, the Classical retreat ...Bg6 is a mistake. White's knight on f3 is ready for Ne5, and with h4 and the queen it hunts the bishop and hits f7.}
1. e4 c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 5. Ng3 Bg6?! {Natural, but now h4 and Ne5 are coming.}
6. h4 {Threatens h5, trapping the bishop.} h6 7. Ne5 {Hits the bishop again.} Bh7? {Natural, but now f7 is weak. 7...Qd6 is Black's best try.}
8. Qh5! {[%cal Gh5f7,Ge5f7] Threatens Qxf7 mate.} g6 {Black's best defense. 8...Qd5? 9.Bc4 hits the queen and adds a third attacker on f7 (Stockfish +5.4, winning for White).}
9. Qf3 {[%cal Gf3f7,Ge5f7] The queen steps out of the attack and threatens Qxf7 mate again. After 9...Nf6, 10.Qb3 hits b7 and f7 at once (Stockfish +1.6, clearly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Trap, Two Knights Attack: 7...h6?? 8.Nxf7!"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "8.Nxf7"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Two Knights Attack"]
[ECO "B10"]

{After 4...Nd7 5.Bc4 Ngf6 6.Neg5 e6 7.Qe2, three White pieces aim at e6 and f7. If Black kicks the knight with ...h6 now, the knight takes on f7.}
1. e4 c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 5. Bc4 Ngf6 6. Neg5 e6 7. Qe2 h6?? {Kicks the knight, but it is too late.}
8. Nxf7! {[%cal Gg5f7] Forks the queen and the rook on h8.} Qc7 {Saves the queen. 8...Kxf7?? 9.Qxe6+ Kg6 10.Bd3+ Kh5 11.Qh3 is mate.}
9. Nxh8 {White has won the rook and a pawn (Stockfish +7.0, winning for White).} *

[Chapter "Trap to avoid, Maróczy Variation: 5.exd5? against 3...e5"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Maróczy Variation"]
[ECO "B12"]

{Some Black players answer the Maróczy (Fantasy) with 3...e5, a gambit with a mating trap. Take the pawn, then refute it with 5.Nc3! and 6.Na4.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 {This is the Maróczy Variation, also called the Fantasy.} e5 {Opens the dark diagonals toward White's king.}
4. dxe5 {Takes the pawn.} Qb6 {[%cal Rb6g1] Eyes g1 and prepares ...Bc5. 4...Bc5 5.Nc3 Qb6 6.Na4 comes to the same thing.}
5. Nc3! {Develops and prepares Na4, which will hit the queen and the bishop.}
(5. exd5? Bc5 {Now g1 is under fire.} 6. Ne2?? Bf2+ 7. Kd2 Qe3# {Mate. This is the trap Black is hoping for.})
5... Bc5 {If 5...dxe4, then 6.Nxe4 Bf5 7.Nd6+ Bxd6 8.exd6 gives White the bishop pair and a strong pawn on d6 (Stockfish +0.8).}
6. Na4! {[%cal Ga4b6,Ga4c5] Hits the queen and the bishop at once.} Bf2+ {If 6...Qa5+, then 7.c3 Bxg1 8.Rxg1 leaves White a pawn up with the bishop pair.}
7. Ke2 {The king steps up, but Black has no follow-up.} Qd4 8. Qxd4 Bxd4 9. f4 {White keeps the extra pawn (Stockfish +0.6, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Trap to avoid, Karpov Variation: 5...Ngf6?? 6.Nd6#"]
[Diagram "5.Qe2"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Karpov Variation"]
[ECO "B17"]

{If you play Karpov Variation: 4...Nd7, know this trap. With White's queen on e2, the e7 pawn is pinned to the king, so a knight that lands on d6 can't be taken.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7
5. Qe2 {[%cal Ge4d6,Ge2e7] Sets the trap.} Ndf6! {Moves the d7 knight, so the king gets d7 as an escape square, and challenges the e4 knight.}
(5... Ngf6?? {The natural move, and it loses at once.} 6. Nd6# {Smothered mate. The e7 pawn is pinned, and Black's own pieces box in the king.})
6. Nf3 {If 6.Nxf6+, take back with the knight and develop normally.} Nxe4 7. Qxe4 Nf6 {Black has traded off the e4 knight and gains time on the queen (Stockfish +0.4, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Trap to avoid, Rasa-Studier Gambit: 6...Nf6? 7.Bxf7+"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "4.f3"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Rasa-Studier Gambit"]
[ECO "B15"]

{Some White players gamble with 3.Nc3 dxe4 4.f3. Take the pawn and develop calmly, but once your bishop is on g4 and White's is on c4, play ...e6 before ...Nf6.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. f3 {This is the Rasa-Studier Gambit.} exf3 {Accept: the extra pawn is safe.}
5. Nxf3 Bg4 {Pins the knight.}
6. Bc4 {[%cal Gc4f7] Aims at f7. If 6.h3, take on f3 and grab a second pawn with ...Qxd4.} e6! {Shuts the bishop off from f7.}
(6... Nf6? 7. Bxf7+! Kxf7 8. Ne5+ {Forks the king and the bishop on g4 (Stockfish +1.9, clearly better for White).})
7. O-O Be7 8. h3 Bh5 {Black is a pawn up with a solid position (Stockfish −1.0, clearly better for Black).} *

[Chapter "Trap, Maróczy Variation: 5.dxe5? Qh4+"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "5.dxe5"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Maróczy Variation"]
[ECO "B12"]

{After 3.f3 dxe4 4.fxe4 e5, the pawn on e5 looks free. It isn't: f3 has opened the e1-h4 diagonal to White's king.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 {This is the Maróczy Variation, also called the Fantasy.} dxe4 4. fxe4 e5
5. dxe5? {Grabs the pawn.} Qh4+! {[%cal Gh4e1,Gh4e4] Check, and the queen eyes the loose pawn on e4.}
6. Kd2 {White's best try. 6.g3? Qxe4+ forks the king and the rook on h1 (Stockfish −3.3, winning for Black).} Qf4+
7. Ke1 Qxe4+ {Wins the pawn back with check.} 8. Qe2 Bf5 {White's king can no longer castle, and the pawn on e5 is weak (Stockfish −1.1, clearly better for Black).} *

[Chapter "Trap, Maróczy Variation: 6.Ne2?? Qe3#"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Maróczy Variation"]
[ECO "B12"]

{A surprise weapon against the Maróczy (Fantasy): Black gives a pawn to open the dark diagonals that f3 weakened, and sets a mating trap. It is risky, because against 5.Nc3! and 6.Na4 White is slightly better (Stockfish +0.5).}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 e5 {[%cal Gd8h4,Gf8c5] Opens lines toward e1 and g1.}
4. dxe5 {The most common reply.}
(4. exd5 exd4 5. Qxd4 cxd5 {Black's development is easy (Stockfish 0.0, equal).})
4... Qb6 {[%cal Gb6g1] Eyes g1 and b2, and makes room for ...Bc5.}
5. exd5 {Grabs a second pawn. The best defense is 5.Nc3! Bc5 6.Na4!, hitting the queen and the bishop.} Bc5 {[%cal Gc5g1,Gb6g1] Two pieces hit g1.}
6. Ne2?? {The most common reply, and it loses at once. Only 6.Nh3! keeps White in the game (Stockfish −0.3, slightly better for Black).} Bf2+ 7. Kd2 Qe3# {Mate. The king has no squares.} *

[Chapter "Advance, Main line, Tal Variation: 4.h4"]
[Side "both"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Advance Variation, Tal Variation"]
[ECO "B12"]

{White chases the bishop with the h-pawn before developing. Black stops the pawn with ...h5, trades the light-squared bishops and attacks d4 as usual.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5
4. h4 {This is the Tal Variation: h5 next would drive the bishop back.} h5 {Stops the pawn. In return, g5 becomes a good square for White's pieces.}
5. Bd3 {Offers the bishop trade.} Bxd3 6. Qxd3 e6 {With the bishops off, ...e6 shuts nothing in.}
7. Nf3 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits the base of the chain.}
8. c3 Nc6 9. O-O cxd4 10. cxd4 Nge7 {[%cal Ge7f5] The knight heads for f5 to hit d4 (Stockfish +0.2, equal).} *

[Chapter "Advance, Main line, Bayonet Attack: 4.g4"]
[Side "both"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Advance Variation, Bayonet Attack"]
[ECO "B12"]

{White chases the bishop with the g- and h-pawns at once. The play is sharp: White gives pawns to open lines toward Black's king.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5
4. g4 {This is the Bayonet Attack.} Bg6
5. h4 {Threatens h5, trapping the bishop.} h5 {Stops h5.}
6. e6 {A pawn sacrifice that opens lines toward Black's king.} fxe6
7. Bd3 {Offers the bishop trade.} Bxd3 8. Qxd3 hxg4 {Takes a second pawn.}
9. Qg6+ Kd7 {The king is safe enough on d7.}
10. Qxg4 {White wins a pawn back. Black is a pawn up, but the king can no longer castle (Stockfish +0.2, equal).} *

[Chapter "Advance, Main line, Van der Wiel Attack: 4.Nc3"]
[Side "both"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Advance Variation, Van der Wiel Attack"]
[ECO "B12"]

{White develops the queen's knight first and keeps the option of a quick g4. Black strikes at d4 with ...c5 as usual.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5
4. Nc3 {This is the Van der Wiel Attack.} e6
5. Nf3 {The quiet move.}
(5. g4 {The sharp line: White chases the bishop.} Bg6 6. Nge2 c5 7. h4 {Threatens h5, trapping the bishop (Stockfish 0.0, equal).})
5... c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits d4.}
6. Bb5+ Nc6 7. O-O Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] Pins the knight that guards d4.}
8. h3 Bxf3 9. Qxf3 cxd4 10. Ne2 {The knight steps away from the attack and eyes d4 (Stockfish 0.0, equal).} *

[Chapter "Advance, Botvinnik-Carls Defense: 4.dxc5"]
[Side "both"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Advance Variation, Botvinnik-Carls Defense"]
[ECO "B12"]

{Instead of 4.c3, White takes the pawn. Black wins it back with ...e6 and ...Bxc5, and White gets quick development.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 {This is the Botvinnik-Carls Defense.}
4. dxc5 {Takes the pawn.} e6 {Black's best: ...Bxc5 will win the pawn back.}
(4... Nc6 5. f4! {Supports e5 and keeps the extra pawn for now.} e6 6. Be3 d4 7. Bf2 Bxc5 8. Nd2 {Black has won back the pawn, but White develops fast and d4 can become a target (Stockfish +0.8, slightly better for White).})
5. Nf3 Bxc5 {Against 5...Nd7, 6.c4 hits d5 at once (Stockfish +0.7).}
6. Bd3 Nc6 7. O-O Nge7
8. a3 {Prepares b4, gaining space with a gain of time on the bishop.} a6 9. Qe2 O-O
10. Rd1 {White has easy development and pressure on d5 (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Advance, Botvinnik-Carls Defense: White's other fourth moves"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "4.Nf3"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Advance Variation, Botvinnik-Carls Defense"]
[ECO "B12"]

{Against 4.Nf3, take on d4 at once. Against 4.c4, develop first, then take on d4.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 {This is the Botvinnik-Carls Defense.}
4. Nf3 {Develops, but leaves d4 without pawn support.}
(4. c4 {Hits d5 from the side.} Nc6 5. Nf3 cxd4 6. Nxd4 e6 7. Nc3 Bc5 {Develops with a gain of time on the knight (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White).})
4... cxd4 {Take at once: White can't take back with a pawn.}
5. Qxd4 Nc6 {Gains time on the queen.} 6. Qf4 e6
7. Bd3 Nb4! {[%cal Gb4d3] Goes after White's good bishop.}
8. Nc3 Nxd3+ 9. cxd3 Ne7 {Black has the bishop pair, and ...Bd7 and ...Nc6 or ...Nf5 come next (Stockfish +0.5, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Exchange Variation: 2.Nf3 and 4.d4"]
[Side "both"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Diagram "4.d4"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Exchange Variation"]
[ECO "B13"]

{In club games this Exchange position, with White's knight on f3, comes more often from 2.Nf3 than from 2.d4 d5 3.exd5 cxd5 4.Nf3. Black develops with ...Nc6 and pins the f3 knight with ...Bg4.}
1. e4 c6 {Prepares ...d5.}
2. Nf3 {Develops and keeps the choice between d4 and d3.} d5
3. exd5 {White's most common choice after 2.Nf3.} cxd5 {Black recaptures toward the center.}
4. d4 {This is the Exchange Variation, the same position as 2.d4 d5 3.exd5 cxd5 4.Nf3.} Nc6 {[%cal Gc6d4] Hits d4 and prepares ...Bg4.}
5. Bb5 {Pins the knight.} Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] Pins the knight that guards d4.}
6. O-O e6 7. h3 Bxf3 {Black gives up the bishop rather than retreat.}
8. Qxf3 Nf6 9. Bg5 Be7 10. Nc3 O-O
11. Bxc6 bxc6 {[%cal Gc6d5] The c6 pawn guards d5, and Black's rook can use the half-open b-file (Stockfish 0.0, equal).} *

[Chapter "Exchange Variation: 4.Bd3"]
[Side "both"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Exchange Variation, Rubinstein Variation"]
[ECO "B13"]

{White trades on d5 and aims the pieces at Black's kingside. Black develops, trades White's best bishop and plays on the queenside with ...b5 and ...b4.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 {Black recaptures toward the center.}
4. Bd3 {Develops and keeps Black's bishop off f5. 4.Nf3 is under Exchange Variation: 2.Nf3 and 4.d4.} Nc6 {[%cal Gc6d4] Hits d4.}
5. c3 Nf6
6. Bf4 {This is the Rubinstein Variation: the bishops on d3 and f4 aim at Black's kingside.} Bg4 {Develops with a threat against the queen.}
7. Qb3 {Attacks b7.} Qd7 {Guards b7.}
8. Nd2 e6 9. Ngf3 Bd6 {Offers to trade White's active bishop.}
10. Bxd6 Qxd6 11. O-O O-O {[%cal Gb7b5,Gb5b4] Black follows with ...b5 and ...b4, the minority attack (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Modern Variation: 3.Nd2"]
[Side "both"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Classical Variation"]
[ECO "B18"]

{3.Nd2 defends e4 just as 3.Nc3 does, and after 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 the position is the same. Black plays the Classical Variation with 4...Bf5.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 {This is the Modern Variation. It does the same job as 3.Nc3.} dxe4
4. Nxe4 Bf5 {The same position as Classical Variation: 4...Bf5 (Stockfish +0.4, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Classical Variation, Main Line: 6.h4"]
[Side "both"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Classical Variation, Lobron System"]
[ECO "B19"]

{Instead of 6.Nf3, White chases the bishop with the h-pawn. Black gives it h7 with ...h6, trades it on d3, and gets a solid position with no weaknesses.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 5. Ng3 Bg6
6. h4 {[%cal Gh4h5] This is the Main Line: h5 would drive the bishop off g6.} h6! {Makes room for the bishop on h7. The only move that keeps the balance.}
7. Nf3 {7.h5 Bh7 8.Nf3 Nd7 reaches the main line by another move order.} Nd7 {Guards e5, so White's knight can't jump there.}
8. h5 {[%csl Yh5,Yh6] This is the Spassky Variation: White gains space and fixes the h6 pawn. Against 8.Bd3, Black takes first: 8...Bxd3 9.Qxd3 e6.} Bh7
9. Bd3 {Offers to trade the bishop that Black has moved three times.} Bxd3
10. Qxd3 e6 {[%cal Gc6d5,Ge6d5] With the bishops off, ...e6 locks nothing in.}
11. Bd2 {White prepares to castle queenside. Against 11.Bf4, Black checks with 11...Qa5+ and develops with ...Bb4 and ...Ngf6.} Ngf6
12. O-O-O Be7 {This is the Lobron System. Black's king can still castle on either side.}
13. Ne4 {Offers a knight trade that brings White's queen to the center.} Nxe4
14. Qxe4 Nf6 {Gains time on the queen.}
15. Qe2 Qd5 {[%cal Gd5a2] Eyes a2 and offers a queen trade on e4.}
16. c4 Qe4 {Black offers the trade anyway.}
17. Qxe4 Nxe4 {Black's pawns have no weaknesses, and White's h5 pawn can become a target in the endgame (Stockfish +0.4, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Karpov Variation: 4...Nd7"]
[Side "both"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Karpov Variation"]
[ECO "B17"]

{Black prepares ...Ngf6, so that if White trades on f6 a knight takes back and the pawns stay healthy. Black must know the trap 5.Qe2 Ngf6?? 6.Nd6#, in the Traps section.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7
5. Nf3 {The most natural move. Against 5.Ng5 or 5.Bd3, Black develops with ...Ngf6, ...e6 and ...Bd6, and kicks the knight with ...h6 only once f7 is covered.} Ngf6
6. Nxf6+ {White trades, but a knight takes back, so the pawns stay healthy. Against 6.Ng3, Black plays ...e6 and ...c5.} Nxf6
7. Bc4 {Aims at f7 and prepares Ne5.} Bf5 {The bishop gets out first.}
8. O-O e6 9. c3 Bd6 {Black's minor pieces are all out, and castling comes next (Stockfish +0.2, equal).} *

[Chapter "Bronstein-Larsen Variation: 5...gxf6"]
[Side "both"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Bronstein-Larsen Variation"]
[ECO "B16"]

{Black takes back on f6 with the g-pawn. The pawns are broken, but the g-file opens for a rook and the bishops come out fast.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 {Offers the knight trade.}
5. Nxf6+ gxf6 {This is the Bronstein-Larsen Variation. 5...exf6 is the Tartakower Variation.}
6. Nf3 Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] Pins the knight.}
7. Be2 e6 8. h3 Bh5 9. Bf4 Bd6 {Offers to trade White's active bishop. White has the healthier pawns, and Black has open lines on the kingside (Stockfish +0.6, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Two Knights Attack, Mindeno Variation: 3...Bg4"]
[Side "both"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Two Knights Attack, Mindeno Variation, Exchange Line"]
[ECO "B11"]

{Black pins with 3...Bg4 and gives up the bishop when h3 asks. Black's pawns then go to light squares, where White's remaining light-squared bishop can't attack them.}
1. e4 c6 2. Nc3
(2. Nf3 d5 3. Nc3 Bg4 {The same position by another move order.})
2... d5 3. Nf3 Bg4 {This is the Mindeno Variation. Pins the knight.}
4. h3
(4. Be2 Nf6 {Develops and hits e4.} 5. e5 Nfd7 6. d4 e6 {A French structure, but Black's light-squared bishop is already outside the chain (Stockfish +0.2, equal).})
4... Bxf3 {This is the Exchange Line. Black takes when asked.}
(4... Bh5?! {This is the Retreat Line. It keeps the pin but loses time.} 5. exd5 cxd5 6. g4 Bg6 7. Ne5 {White gains space and time against the bishop (Stockfish +0.7, slightly better for White).})
5. Qxf3 e6 {[%csl Yc6,Yd5,Ye6] The pawn wall on light squares.}
6. d4 Nf6 7. Bd3 dxe4 8. Nxe4 Qxd4 {Takes the pawn. Black must finish developing quickly with ...Nbd7, ...Be7 and ...O-O.}
9. O-O Nbd7 {White has development for the pawn (Stockfish +0.1, equal).} *

[Chapter "Two Knights Attack: 4...Bf5 5.Ng3 Bg6?!"]
[Side "white"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Two Knights Attack"]
[ECO "B10"]

{The Classical setup with ...Bf5 works less well here: the f3 knight is ready for Ne5, and h4 gains time against the bishop.}
1. e4 c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5
5. Ng3 {Attacks the bishop.} Bg6?! {The usual retreat in the Classical, but here the f3 knight is ready for Ne5.}
6. h4 {Threatens h5, trapping the bishop.} h6 7. Ne5 {Hits the bishop again.} Qd6 {Black's best try. 7...Bh7? loses to 8.Qh5!: see the Traps section.}
8. d4 {White has gained time against the bishop (Stockfish +1.0, clearly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Two Knights Attack: 4...Nd7 and 4...Bg4"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "4...Nd7"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Two Knights Attack"]
[ECO "B10"]

{Black's rarer fourth moves. Against 4...Nd7, White aims everything at e6 and f7; against 4...Bg4, the h-pawn gains time and White takes the bishop pair.}
1. e4 c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7
(4... Bg4 5. h3 Bh5 6. Ng3 Bxf3 7. Qxf3 e6 8. Be2 {White has the bishop pair (Stockfish +0.4, slightly better for White).})
5. Bc4 {Aims at f7.} Ngf6 6. Neg5 {Now e6 and f7 are both targets.} e6
7. Qe2 {[%cal Gg5f7,Gc4f7,Ge2e6] Three pieces aim at e6 and f7. 7...h6?? loses to 8.Nxf7!: see the Traps section.} Nd5 {Black's best: it blocks the bishop's diagonal.}
(7... Nb6 8. Ne5! {[%cal Gg5f7,Ge5f7] Two knights hit f7.} Nxc4 9. Qxc4 Qd5 10. Qxd5 exd5 11. Nexf7 {White wins the f7 pawn (Stockfish +0.7, slightly better for White).})
8. d4 Be7 9. O-O {White's pieces are more active (Stockfish +0.6, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Two Knights Attack: 3...d4?!"]
[Side "white"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Two Knights Attack"]
[ECO "B10"]

{A common mistake against the Two Knights. 3...d4?! gains space with a gain of time, but c3 breaks up the pawn at once.}
1. e4 c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 d4?! {Gains space with a gain of time, but the pawn becomes a target.}
4. Ne2 c5 5. c3! {Strikes at the pawn at once.} dxc3 6. bxc3 Nf6 7. Ng3 Nc6
8. Bc4 {White has a strong center and quick development (Stockfish +1.0, clearly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Maróczy Variation: 5...Bg4 6.c3"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "5...Bg4"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Maróczy Variation"]
[ECO "B12"]

{Instead of taking on d4, Black pins the knight that guards it. Support d4 with c3, aim the bishop at f7, castle, and bring the queen to the kingside via e1.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 {This is the Maróczy Variation, also called the Fantasy.} dxe4 4. fxe4 e5 5. Nf3 Bg4 {Pins the knight that guards d4.}
6. c3 {Supports d4, so Black can't win it.} Nf6
7. Bc4 {Aims at f7.} Bh5 8. O-O Nbd7
9. Qe1 {[%cal Ge1h4] Unpins the knight, and the queen heads for h4 or g3 (Stockfish +0.4, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Maróczy Variation: White's other fifth moves"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "4...e5"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Maróczy Variation"]
[ECO "B12"]

{White's rarer fifth moves instead of 5.Nf3 in Maróczy Variation: 3...dxe4. Against 5.Be3, the queen check on h4 wins a pawn; against 5.Nc3, take on d4.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 {This is the Maróczy Variation, also called the Fantasy.} dxe4 4. fxe4 e5
5. Be3
(5. Nc3? exd4 {Hits the knight.} 6. Nce2 Nf6 {Black is a clean pawn up, and e4 is weak too (Stockfish −1.6, clearly better for Black).})
5... Qh4+ {The weakened diagonal.} 6. Bf2 Qxe4+ 7. Qe2 Qxe2+ 8. Nxe2 exd4 {Black is a pawn up in the endgame (Stockfish −0.8, slightly better for Black).} *

[Chapter "Maróczy Variation, Maróczy Gambit: 5...exd4"]
[Side "white"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Maróczy Variation, Maróczy Gambit"]
[ECO "B12"]

{After 3...dxe4 4.fxe4, White has two center pawns and the half-open f-file. Against 5...exd4, White gives the d-pawn for fast development and pressure on f7.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 {This is the Maróczy Variation, also called the Fantasy.} dxe4 4. fxe4 e5 {Black's best: it hits d4 at once.}
(4... Nf6? 5. e5 {Kicks the knight and gains space.} Nd5 6. c4 {The knight must retreat again (Stockfish +1.4, clearly better for White).})
5. Nf3 {Develops and attacks e5. Never 5.dxe5?, which runs into 5...Qh4+.} exd4 {Black takes the pawn.}
6. Bc4 {This is the Maróczy Gambit: White gives the d-pawn for fast development.} Nf6 7. O-O Nbd7
8. Qe1 {The queen heads for g3 or h4 (Stockfish +0.8, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Maróczy Variation: 3...g6"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "3...g6"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Maróczy Variation"]
[ECO "B12"]

{Black can also fianchetto with 3...g6. White develops quickly with Nc3 and gains space with e5.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 {This is the Maróczy Variation, also called the Fantasy.} g6
4. Nc3 Bg7 5. e5 {Gains space and blunts the g7 bishop.} f6 6. f4 {Supports e5 (Stockfish +0.4, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Sidelines: Hillbilly Attack 2.Bc4 and Accelerated Panov 2.c4"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "2.Bc4"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Hillbilly Attack"]
[ECO "B10"]

{Against White's rarer second moves, Black still plays ...d5 and gets an easy game.}
1. e4 c6
2. Bc4 {This is the Hillbilly Attack. The bishop runs into ...d5.}
(2. c4 {This is the Accelerated Panov Attack.} d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. cxd5 Nf6 {The knight will take back on d5.} 5. Nc3 Nxd5 6. Nf3 Nc6 {Black's development is easy (Stockfish +0.1, equal).})
2... d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. Bb5+ Bd7 {Offers the bishop trade (Stockfish −0.4, slightly better for Black).} *

[Chapter "Sidelines, Breyer Variation: 2.d3"]
[Side "both"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Breyer Variation"]
[ECO "B10"]

{2.d3 is the King's Indian Attack setup: d3, Nd2, Ngf3, g3, Bg2 and O-O. With White's pawn on d3, Black can take a big center with ...d5 and ...e5.}
1. e4 c6 2. d3 {This is the Breyer Variation.} d5 3. Nd2 e5 {Black takes a big center.}
4. Ngf3 Bd6 5. g3 Nf6 6. Bg2 O-O 7. O-O Re8 {Black has a fine center and well-placed pieces (Stockfish −0.2, equal).} *

